# Normalise line endings to LF everywhere.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows checks out CRLF (core.autocrlf defaults to true
# there, including on GitHub's windows-latest runners), while biome.json sets
# "lineEnding": "lf". The result is that `pnpm lint` fails on *every* text file
# on Windows — not a few, all of them — which made the Windows CI job
# unpassable and therefore blocked any Windows release.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` forces LF in the working
# tree on every platform. Files are stored as LF in the repository either way,
# so this changes nothing for existing Linux and macOS checkouts.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must keep CRLF to work on Windows.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary — never subject to EOL conversion. `text=auto` detects most of these,
# but being explicit avoids a mis-detected file being silently corrupted on
# checkout, which is a miserable thing to diagnose.
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.gif      binary
*.ico      binary
*.icns     binary
*.webp     binary
*.avif     binary
*.pdf      binary
*.woff     binary
*.woff2    binary
*.ttf      binary
*.otf      binary
*.wasm     binary
*.so       binary
*.dylib    binary
*.dll      binary
*.exe      binary
*.zip      binary
*.gz       binary
*.tgz      binary
*.AppImage binary
*.deb      binary
*.rpm      binary
*.dmg      binary

# ONNX models are committed directly (small bundled models) or downloaded
# at runtime (large models from release assets / upstream URLs).  No Git
# LFS dependency — the free-tier budget was exhausted by the 980 MB
# ddcolor source, and Release assets have no bandwidth quota.

# Generated lockfiles: collapsed in diffs and excluded from language stats,
# without being hidden from review.
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true
Cargo.lock     linguist-generated=true
